WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.888
Sorting brings equal and nearby values together so global relationships become local checks.

00:00:06.888 --> 00:00:13.327
Python's built-in Timsort runs in O(N log N) and exploits existing order in the input.

00:00:13.327 --> 00:00:17.520
Most sweep-line and greedy algorithms begin with a sort.

00:00:17.817 --> 00:00:22.804
Pass a key function that maps each item to a lightweight comparison tuple.

00:00:22.804 --> 00:00:28.196
Negate numeric fields when you need descending order on a secondary tie-breaker.

00:00:28.196 --> 00:00:32.577
Avoid slow comparator functions when a direct tuple key suffices.

00:00:32.867 --> 00:00:38.374
Convert intervals into start and end events, then sort all events by coordinate.

00:00:38.374 --> 00:00:42.436
Maintain an active counter as you sweep from left to right.

00:00:42.436 --> 00:00:48.907
Order tie-breaking deltas carefully depending on whether endpoints are inclusive or exclusive.

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:57.133
When values reach a billion but the count of items is small, replace each number with its rank in sorted unique order.

00:00:57.133 --> 00:01:02.108
Coordinate compression maps sparse values into a compact zero-based array.

00:01:02.108 --> 00:01:06.411
Data structures like Fenwick trees can then index them directly.
